# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets -- never commit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors / OS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT ignored, on purpose:
#   uv.lock                          -- reproducible installs
#   .reports/step-5c5/               -- see below
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Locally derived data -- regenerable, and large
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stored evidence and normalised document artifacts from a real pilot run, and the
# reviewer manifests generated from them. All three are reproduced by a documented
# command (`make extract-documents`, `make claims-run`, `make verify-manifest`) and
# none of them is a source of truth, so committing them would only add churn.
.evidence-*/
.artifacts-*/

# `.reports/*` rather than `.reports/`: git does not descend into an ignored
# DIRECTORY, so a negation underneath one can never match. Ignoring the contents
# instead leaves the directory traversable, which is what makes the exception below
# work at all. Both patterns are `**/`-prefixed because the reports live under
# apps/api, and a pattern with an interior slash is anchored to this file's directory.
**/.reports/*

# ...with ONE exception. `verification/domain_binding.py` and `verification/console.py`
# hard-code the sha256 of these three manifests, and every responsibility decision and
# promotion is refused unless the bytes on disk hash to the approved value. That makes
# them an INPUT the code is pinned to, not derived output: a deployment without them
# cannot verify anything, and the console's manifest panel reports them missing. The
# ignore rule above is about size and churn, and 1.3MB of approved review packages is
# neither.
!**/.reports/step-5c5/
